# /i/HOUSE7 is a path, not a file. One page reads the code out of the URL, so there is
# nothing to generate per event and nothing to rebuild when an event is created.
#
# 200, not 301: a redirect would change the URL in the address bar and, more importantly,
# Apple's universal-link matching runs against the URL as SENT. Rewriting keeps /i/CODE.
# THE DESTINATION IS `/i/`, NOT `/i/index.html`. Pages canonicalises the explicit filename
# -- `/i/index.html` answers 308 to `/i/` -- and a rewrite whose target redirects does not
# serve, so `/i/SR1017` 404'd on the first deploy with the file spelled out. Measured, not
# guessed: `/i/` returns 200 text/html and `/i/index.html` returns 308.

# THE APP ITSELF, AT THE ROOT OF THIS PROJECT (#78). `expo export` writes ABSOLUTE asset
# paths (`/_expo/static/js/web/entry-*.js`), so the bundle can only be served from a root --
# putting it under `/app/` would need `experiments.baseUrl` in app.json, which is shared with
# the native build and with lane B's harness exports. The root was a 404 before this, so
# nothing was displaced. Measured, not assumed.
#
# A BARE `/*  /  200` BREAKS THE SITE, and this was measured rather than reasoned about. On
# Cloudflare Pages a `_redirects` rule takes precedence over a real static file at the same
# path -- the opposite of the assumption -- so the catch-all served the app's HTML shell for
# `/_expo/static/js/web/entry-*.js` (1172 bytes of HTML where a 2.8MB bundle should be) and
# for `/.well-known/assetlinks.json`, which is the file Android checks at INSTALL time to
# decide whether this app may open an https link. The app booted to nothing and the
# association was silently dead, under three green 200s.
#
# So every real directory is passed through FIRST. A rule mapping a path to itself resolves
# to the static asset and stops the list. Order is top to bottom, first match wins:
#   `/i/*` before everything, or the app's own `/i/[code]` route renders to somebody who has
#   not installed it -- the opposite of what that page exists for.
/i/*             /i/                        200
/_expo/*         /_expo/:splat              200
# CLOUDFLARE PAGES SILENTLY SKIPS ANY PATH CONTAINING `node_modules`, and expo puts 18
# router assets under `assets/node_modules/expo-router/assets/`. They never left the machine
# on the first deploy -- wrangler reported "Uploaded 4 files" against 26 staged and said
# nothing about the other 18. The bundle references them by absolute path, and the
# unmatched-route screen that uses them IS reachable because the catch-all below renders the
# app for every unknown path. `tools/deploy-web.mjs` stages them as `assets/_nm/`; this maps
# the name the bundle asks for onto the name Cloudflare will accept. Must precede `/assets/*`.
/assets/node_modules/*  /assets/_nm/:splat  200
/assets/*        /assets/:splat             200
/.well-known/*   /.well-known/:splat        200
/metadata.json   /metadata.json             200
/*               /                          200
